WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.110
Tap, tap.

2
00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:04.990
A telegraph key sends
letters as dots and dashes.

3
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.890
E is a single dot.

4
00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:08.910
T is a single dash.

5
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.790
A is dot dash.

6
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:11.810
And Q?

7
00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.010
Dash, dash, dot, dash.

8
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:19.990
Store the same letters in 7-bit ASCII,
and every letter takes exactly 7 bits.

9
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.390
So why does Morse give
E just one tiny dot,

10
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.770
while Q needs four symbols?

11
00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:30.630
In this mission, you'll compare fixed-size
codes with codes of different lengths.

12
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.790
You'll see how shorter codes
mean fewer bits to send or store.

13
00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.910
And you'll meet Huffman coding, which
builds codes of different lengths from a

14
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:39.910
tree.

15
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.720
Your mission: explain why Morse code
gives the letter E a single dot.
